INSERT INTO "BlogPost" (id, "storeId", slug, title, excerpt, content, "coverImage", published, "publishedAt", "updatedAt")
VALUES (
  'cm_scs_coshh_backfill_0001',
  'cms4ll4ky0001a99vuky7muf7',
  'coshh-cleaning-chemicals-guide',
  'COSHH and Cleaning Chemicals: What Every Staffordshire Business Must Know',
  'If your business uses cleaning chemicals — and almost every business does — you are legally required to manage the risks under COSHH. Here is what that means in practice and how to stay compliant without overcomplicating it.',
  '<p>If your business uses cleaning chemicals, COSHH applies to you. The Control of Substances Hazardous to Health Regulations 2002 cover any substance that could harm health — and that includes many everyday cleaning products, from bleach and degreasers to disinfectants and descalers. Businesses across Staffordshire, from care homes in Stafford to hotels in Stoke-on-Trent, are legally required to assess and manage these risks. The good news is that with the right approach, COSHH compliance is straightforward.</p>

<h2>What COSHH Means for Cleaning Products</h2>
<p>COSHH requires employers to identify which substances employees are exposed to, assess the risks, and put controls in place to protect people. For cleaning operations, this means understanding which products contain hazardous ingredients and ensuring they are used, stored, and disposed of correctly. Safety data sheets — sometimes called MSDS or SDS — are the starting point. Every cleaning chemical you purchase should come with one, and suppliers are legally required to provide them.</p>
<p>Safety data sheets set out the hazard classification, first aid measures, storage requirements, and personal protective equipment (PPE) needed for each product. If you are buying cleaning supplies from a reputable trade supplier, these documents should be readily available, either in print or for download.</p>

<h2>Carrying Out a COSHH Assessment</h2>
<p>A COSHH assessment does not need to be a lengthy document. For most cleaning operations, a straightforward written record covering each chemical in use is sufficient. Your assessment should cover the following for each product:</p>
<ul>
<li>What the substance is and what it is used for</li>
<li>Who could be exposed and how (inhalation, skin contact, ingestion)</li>
<li>What control measures are in place (dilution, ventilation, PPE)</li>
<li>What to do in an emergency</li>
<li>How often the assessment will be reviewed</li>
</ul>
<p>For most businesses, the hazard information on the safety data sheet feeds directly into this assessment. The HSE provides a free COSHH assessment tool on its website for businesses that want a guided approach.</p>

<h2>Dilution and Control Measures</h2>
<p>One of the most effective ways to reduce risk from cleaning chemicals is correct dilution. Many concentrated products are significantly more hazardous in their undiluted form — skin and respiratory irritation risks drop sharply when products are mixed to the recommended use concentration. This is one reason why dosing systems and pre-measured sachets are popular in commercial settings: they reduce both chemical exposure and product waste.</p>
<p>Ventilation matters too. Using strong cleaning chemicals in poorly ventilated spaces — walk-in fridges, basement plant rooms, small toilets — increases the risk of inhalation. Where ventilation cannot be improved, respiratory PPE may be required. Your safety data sheets will specify the minimum PPE for each product.</p>

<h2>Training and Record-Keeping</h2>
<p>COSHH requires that employees who use hazardous substances are properly trained. This does not mean a formal course for every cleaner, but it does mean they should understand the risks of the products they use, how to dilute them correctly, what PPE to use, and what to do if there is a spillage or skin contact. Brief toolbox talks, backed up by laminated product information sheets near the storage area, are a practical and widely used approach.</p>
<p>Keep a record of your assessments and any training provided. In the event of an HSE inspection or an incident, this documentation demonstrates that you have taken your legal duty seriously.</p>

<h2>Choosing Products That Make Compliance Easier</h2>
<p>Selecting the right cleaning products in the first place can simplify your COSHH obligations considerably. Products with lower hazard classifications, clear dilution guidance, and readily available safety data sheets reduce the administrative burden. Buying from a specialist trade supplier means you get proper documentation as standard, rather than having to chase it down from a generalist wholesaler.</p>
<p>It is also worth reviewing your product range periodically. Using fewer, multi-purpose products rather than a large number of specialist chemicals can reduce the number of COSHH assessments you need to maintain.</p>

<p>If you would like guidance on choosing cleaning chemicals that are effective, compliant, and properly documented, <a href="/contact">get in touch</a> — or <a href="/products">browse our full range</a>, where safety data sheets are available for every product we stock.</p>',
  NULL,
  true,
  '2026-08-28T09:00:00Z',
  '2026-08-28T09:00:00Z'
);

INSERT INTO "BlogPost" (id, "storeId", slug, title, excerpt, content, "coverImage", published, "publishedAt", "updatedAt")
VALUES (
  'cm_scs_trade_backfill_0002',
  'cms4ll4ky0001a99vuky7muf7',
  'trade-cleaning-accounts-benefits',
  'Why a Trade Cleaning Account Could Save Your Business Time and Money',
  'A trade cleaning account gives businesses fixed pricing, consolidated invoicing, and direct access to a knowledgeable supplier — without the hassle of placing individual orders every time stock runs low. Here is what to look for and whether it is right for your organisation.',
  '<p>For businesses that buy cleaning supplies regularly — whether you manage a care home, run a facilities team, or operate a multi-site hospitality business — a trade cleaning account is worth considering seriously. Rather than placing individual orders at consumer prices, a trade account gives you access to wholesale cleaning supplies pricing, consolidated monthly invoicing, and a direct line to a supplier who understands your needs. Businesses across Staffordshire are increasingly moving to this model, and the reasons are straightforward.</p>

<h2>Consistent Pricing You Can Budget Around</h2>
<p>One of the biggest advantages of a trade account is pricing stability. Consumer and online marketplace prices for cleaning products fluctuate constantly — promotional pricing disappears, products get delisted, and bulk discounts vary. A trade account fixes your pricing for an agreed period, making it far easier to forecast your cleaning supply spend and hold suppliers accountable.</p>
<p>This matters particularly for businesses with tight margins, such as contract cleaning companies or hospitality operators who need to cost their services accurately. Knowing that your degreasers, disinfectants, and janitorial consumables will cost the same next month as they do today removes a source of budget uncertainty.</p>

<h2>Simplified Ordering and Invoicing</h2>
<p>Trade accounts typically replace multiple individual transactions with a single monthly invoice covering everything you have ordered. For finance teams and sole traders alike, this is a significant administrative simplification. Instead of reconciling dozens of receipts and online orders, you receive one document at the end of the month.</p>
<p>Many trade suppliers also offer repeat order facilities — where standing orders for routine consumables are despatched automatically on a schedule. This is particularly useful for products you know you will always need: hand soap, paper towels, refuse sacks, and general-purpose disinfectants. Automating routine replenishment means your team is not spending time re-ordering stock that should simply always be there.</p>

<h2>Access to the Right Products, Not Just Whatever Is in Stock</h2>
<p>Buying cleaning products through a trade account from a specialist supplier means you get access to a properly curated range, including products that are not typically available through consumer channels. Professional-grade disinfectants with EN-tested efficacy, concentrated products with accurate dilution guidance, and sector-specific solutions for healthcare, food service, or education are all available through trade channels in a way they simply are not through general retailers.</p>
<p>A good trade supplier will also hold safety data sheets for every product and can advise on COSHH compliance, dilution ratios, and which product is genuinely appropriate for a given surface or contamination risk. That kind of product knowledge is worth a great deal if you are responsible for infection control in a healthcare setting or chemical safety in a food preparation environment.</p>

<h2>What to Look for in a Trade Supplier</h2>
<p>Not all trade accounts are equal. When evaluating a supplier, consider:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Range depth</strong> — can they supply everything you need, or will you still be buying from multiple sources?</li>
<li><strong>Documentation</strong> — are safety data sheets readily available for every product?</li>
<li><strong>Delivery terms</strong> — what are the minimum order values and lead times?</li>
<li><strong>Product knowledge</strong> — can the supplier give you genuine advice, or are they simply taking orders?</li>
<li><strong>Credit terms</strong> — does the payment schedule work for your business cash flow?</li>
</ul>
<p>For businesses in Staffordshire and the wider Midlands, sourcing from a regional specialist also means faster delivery and a supplier who understands the local market — whether that is the mix of manufacturing sites in the Potteries, care homes across Cannock and Lichfield, or the hospitality businesses around Stafford town centre.</p>

<h2>Is a Trade Account Right for You?</h2>
<p>If your business spends more than a few hundred pounds a month on cleaning supplies, the answer is almost certainly yes. The pricing, administrative, and product knowledge benefits compound quickly, and the setup process is straightforward with a reputable supplier.</p>
<p>If you are spending less than that, it is still worth exploring — many trade accounts have no minimum spend, and even occasional buyers benefit from consistent pricing and proper product documentation.</p>

<p>To find out more about opening a trade account or to discuss your requirements, <a href="/contact">get in touch with our team</a> — or <a href="/products">browse our full product range</a> to see what we stock.</p>',
  NULL,
  true,
  '2026-09-04T09:00:00Z',
  '2026-09-04T09:00:00Z'
);